\section{Compound Sparsity Formulation}
\label{app:formulation-details}

\paragraph{Attention head pruning.}
Heads are ranked by Frobenius norm importance $w_j^{(\ell)} = \|\mathbf{A}_j^{(\ell)}\|_F$ \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}; a binary mask $m_j^{(\ell)} \in \{0,1\}$ retains the top-$(1{-}s)\cdot H$ heads:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\mathbf{o}}_j^{(\ell)} = m_j^{(\ell)} \cdot \mathbf{o}_j^{(\ell)}.
    \label{eq:head-mask}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{KV cache eviction.}
A position mask $\mathbf{m}_k \in \{0,1\}^T$ retains the top-$(1{-}s)\cdot T$ positions (BOS always preserved):
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{a}_{ij} = a_{ij} \cdot m_{k,j}, \quad \forall\, i, j.
    \label{eq:kv-mask}
\end{equation}
Under importance-based eviction, each position $j$ is scored by its cumulative attention mass with position normalization:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{imp}(j) = \frac{\sum_{\ell, h, i} a_{ij}^{(\ell,h)}}{T - j},
    \label{eq:kv-importance}
\end{equation}
where the denominator compensates for causal bias---earlier positions are attended to by more queries \cite{thinkv-iclr2026, defensivekv-iclr2026}. Under random eviction, positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Applying both mechanisms simultaneously yields compound sparsity. The two axes interact: head pruning redistributes attention mass, altering the importance ranking for KV eviction. Prior work \cite{spark-aaai2026} reports super-additive joint effects but treats interaction magnitude as a fixed property of $s$.

\paragraph{Attention sparsity metric.}
For each query position $i$ and head $h$, we compute the fraction of key positions receiving negligible attention:
\begin{equation}
    \sigma_i^{(h)} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{j=1}^{T} \mathbf{1}\!\left[a_{ij}^{(h)} < 0.01 \cdot \max_k a_{ik}^{(h)}\right].
    \label{eq:attn-sparsity}
\end{equation}
The per-position sparsity $\sigma_i = \frac{1}{H}\sum_h \sigma_i^{(h)}$ averages across all heads. Tokens are partitioned into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity phases by the tercile boundaries of $\{\sigma_i\}$; segments shorter than 10 tokens are merged into adjacent phases.

\section{Multiplicative Null Verification}
\label{app:multiplicative-null}

We verified that replacing the additive null $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}) with the multiplicative null $r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$ does not change qualitative conclusions. Under the multiplicative formulation, the interaction index becomes $\mathrm{II}_{\times} = r_b - r_a \cdot r_k$. Across all sparsity levels and both eviction strategies, the sign and relative ordering of $\mathrm{II}_{\times}$ match those of the additive II: importance-based eviction exhibits a monotonically increasing severity gradient ($G_{\times} = 58$--$241{\times}$), while random eviction remains flat ($G_{\times} \approx 1$). The crossover at $s{=}0.25$ is preserved. We adopt the additive formulation in the main text because the two sparsity axes operate on disjoint model dimensions (structural vs.\ positional), making additive independence the more natural null hypothesis at the sparsity levels studied.

\section{Bootstrap and Permutation Details}
\label{app:bootstrap-details}

Per-problem distributions of the paired severity difference $\Delta_i = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under importance-based eviction are right-skewed at all sparsity levels, with mean/median ratios ranging from $1.2{\times}$ ($s{=}0.15$) to $1.6{\times}$ ($s{=}0.30$) for DeepSeek and $1.1{\times}$ to $1.4{\times}$ for Qwen. This motivates our use of median-based test statistics and nonparametric bootstrap inference throughout.

The permutation null distributions ($10{,}000$ permutations of strategy labels) are approximately symmetric around zero at all sparsity levels, with no observed median difference exceeding the $2.5$th or $97.5$th percentile under any permutation at $s \geq 0.20$ (Bonferroni-corrected). At $s{=}0.15$, the observed statistic falls at the $99.68$th percentile of the null ($p_{\text{Bonf}} = 0.0032$).

